#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02281ad3-91b7-507b-9153-43626a0c50bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27x^2-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02281ad3-91b7-507b-9153-43626a0c50bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27x^2-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283199} :answer-field/id #uuid "86178329-4413-5d1c-ba52-5856887faf1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffc80596-d669-5871-b4f4-bb9271ebf7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-274997" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $-27,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})(x+\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{2\\text{i}}{3})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{2\\text{i}}{3},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-27.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-27.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-27({x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}) & =0 \\\\ -27{x}^{2}-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c965408-0661-5df3-80dd-d14773e99e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283202} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7d4fa5-dab8-53c3-8490-4ad457514d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a7d4fa5-dab8-53c3-8490-4ad457514d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283203} :answer-field/id #uuid "e513be72-44dc-592f-974d-272a4db8f365" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1134a1f2-209a-573b-ac08-b8c5a5fffa78" :question/math-academy-id "q-281311" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=8\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=8\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-8\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-8\\text{i})(x+8\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(8\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-64(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+64 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce23700a-2b77-5133-acc6-f6534c983e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16x^2+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce23700a-2b77-5133-acc6-f6534c983e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16x^2+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283206} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3a74b5-9d90-5552-a50a-10d2905522ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0812638e-04de-5eff-afc4-6c171ed3c213" :question/math-academy-id "q-281333" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $16,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{6}{16}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{8} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}}{4},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $16.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $16.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{8}) & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b24ede-2838-5c6b-adbf-6936e5d1c590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+4x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28b24ede-2838-5c6b-adbf-6936e5d1c590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+4x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283209} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a475e4-c705-56a4-a110-996e8c3ddbe6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5a28fcf-c3ae-5a1c-a194-46d9199c5b82" :question/math-academy-id "q-281348" :question/problem "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}))(x-(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})-x(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})+(-2+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i})(-2-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-\\cancel{x\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}}+2x+\\cancel{x\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}}+4-7{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376f4213-4af5-57dd-b9f8-73a4e2a4d3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-2x-16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283212} {:answer/id #uuid "67b76494-66ed-5da4-9641-d4fe09e80ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+8x+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283213} {:answer/id #uuid "78701929-42a9-55ce-814e-5afaddace5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-2x+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283214} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5a5c6a-9401-574c-87ec-9f4443f335b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+16x-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283215} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c734cc-5827-50bf-a344-6686af216bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-16x-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67b76494-66ed-5da4-9641-d4fe09e80ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+8x+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283213} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f8ab07-7280-509c-ad61-e921431e20e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e697c3b-76f6-5723-8d77-8a32e3c28a88" :question/math-academy-id "q-28273" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(-1+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+x-\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+1-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+2x+4) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+8x+16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
